WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:19.252
Big picture-- I showed you
this big picture before.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.520
We're now going to add to it.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.155
Look at what we've done.

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:22.956
Ha!

00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:26.693
Electronic structure all the
way up through [INAUDIBLE],,

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:29.462
[? Louis, ?] molecular
orbital theory,

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.965
how to handle multi-electrons,
all these different kinds

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:32.899
of bonding--

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.834
check.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.503
We've covered every
single one of these,

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:39.239
and this is
solid-state chemistry.

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:41.107
OK, but now here we are, right?

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:43.910
Now OK, remember we
also talked about this

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.912
on the very first lecture.

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:47.814
When you classify solids,
there are two things

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:50.316
that are really important.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.752
One is the bonding type.

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:56.556
How do the atoms in the solid
want to talk to each other?

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.258
How do they interact?

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:01.428
And that is something that we
have covered in a lot of ways.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.497
But the next is how
they come together,

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:07.067
the actual arrangement
of those atoms.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.835
And that is what
we're going to talk

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.838
about today and on Wednesday.

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:16.408
And then after exam two as
we learn about X-rays, that's

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:18.411
a way to characterize
the arrangement

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.780
so that you can see how
they're ordered, OK?

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.549
We're going to use
X-rays and do something

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.451
called X-ray diffraction.

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:28.988
But for this week I want
to just classify it.

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:32.892
I want to talk about
the tools that we

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:36.696
use to understand
how a solid forms,

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:40.600
how to describe the
formation of a solid, how

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.168
the atoms can be described.

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.170
And OK, so, of
course, a solid is

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.340
that which is dimensionally
stable, simple definition,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:47.874
right?

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:52.679
But we can break down
how these atoms arrange

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.348
into two basic categories--

00:01:56.348 --> 00:02:00.453
atomic arrangement, the order,
and what we call it, right?

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:03.957
In the first category,
it's regular.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:07.360
So what that means is
that everywhere I look, I

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.263
have the same thing repeating.

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.164
And you know what?

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:14.734
It repeats over a
long, long way--

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:18.338
like really long, OK?

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:24.344
So that kind of regular, long
range order is a crystal.

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:26.546
That's a crystalline solid.

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:30.617
And that is going to be
the topic of this week,

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:31.851
crystalline solids.

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:35.555
Now, there are also
solids that you can

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:37.991
make where there isn't order.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.560
Or maybe there's
some but not much.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.961
So, things are
kind of jumbled up.

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:45.298
They're not regularly repeating.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:48.101
They might be random,
the atomic arrangement.

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:51.771
Or maybe there's repetition,
but it's very short-range.

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:54.707
So things look ordered, but only
for a few bond lengths, not 10

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:58.344
to the 23rd bond lengths, right?

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:04.350
Those are called amorphous
solids, or also glass.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.754
Now, as I mentioned, this
is the topic of this week,

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:10.557
and this will be
covered on exam two.

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:15.461
This is the topic after
exam two next week, OK?

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:20.667
So we'll talk about both of
these types of solids as we go.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:26.105
All right, now, OK, it
turns out that nature

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:30.610
has been thinking about
order for a long, long time.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.545
And there is a reason for this.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:36.216
Because nature wants
to be efficient.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:39.719
This plant is like, well, I'm
only going to grow this big,

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:42.488
and I got to put as
many seeds as I can

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:44.156
on the surface of this thing.

00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:45.858
How do I fit them--

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.860
how do I pack them in?

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.463
Well, I could fit
them randomly, but it

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:52.932
doesn't seem like I could
get as many seeds on there.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:56.736
What if I packed them in
in some regular pattern?

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.973
What if I packed them
in in some regular way?

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:02.308
Well, you can pack
a lot more in.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.244
And so nature has
already been thinking

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:08.281
about symmetry and order
and even long-range order

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.350
for a long time.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.720
People have been
thinking about this, too.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.391
Robert Hooke, the same person
from Hooke's law, in the 1600s

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:19.826
he was all about cannonballs.

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:22.662
And he was like, how
can I stack cannonballs?

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:24.264
And he tried this, right?

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:29.402
He's like, well, maybe if
I just put them like this,

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:31.671
is that a good
way to stack them?

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:33.673
I don't know.

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:37.977
And then he sort of
put them all together.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:41.614
But now, he tried this, and then
he tried another arrangement,

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.449
and he'd be like,
well, but how--

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.118
first of all, are they stable?

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:46.319
Do they fall over easily?

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:47.320
Because you don't
want cannonballs

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.554
falling all over the place.

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.323
But then also, did
you pack as much

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:52.358
in this way as another way?

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.960
So he was thinking about it.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.764
And he figured out that you
could pack more cannonballs

00:04:57.764 --> 00:05:02.902
in the same area or volume if
you sort of switched it up,

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.203
right?

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:07.507
And then he thought, well, maybe
other things have packing, too.

00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:09.208
And he thought about
all sorts of things.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:11.311
He looked at salt
crystals, and he

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:15.014
said, they must also
pack in some way,

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:18.051
because cannonballs do.

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:23.389
So people have been thinking
about this for a long time, OK?

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:28.127
Now, people are also inspired
by regular order, right?

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:31.564
So if you look at, for
example, an Escher painting,

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:35.268
you can see that there is
a repetitive pattern here.

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:39.305
This is a crystal
of ducks, right?

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:45.345
OK, so now, when you look
at patterns like this,

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:46.279
you have to--

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:49.282
well, you don't have to, but
we have to in this class.

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:53.219
We've got to figure out
what is the repeating unit.

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:56.856
What is it that is
repeating everywhere?

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.691
So that's the first
question we need to ask.

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.060
And you can see if you
look at this picture,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.662
you can draw a box like that.

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:05.765
There's a box around a light
colored, dark colored duck.

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:10.470
And now you see if you take
this box and you move it over,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.806
you translate it over there,
you get exactly the same thing.

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:16.342
If you take that box
and you put it up there,

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:17.643
you get exactly the same thing.

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.312
So that is a repeating unit.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.350
Oh, we have a definition.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.252
It's a unit cell.

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:29.355
It's called a unit cell.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:37.630
And it's the repeating
unit in a crystal.

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:44.604
It's a repeating unit.

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:48.541
Now, it's not always obvious.

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:53.112
So, there's one way that
I can repeat and take

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:55.715
this and make the picture
by repeating it everywhere.

00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:56.549
But check this out--

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:58.351
I could have done that.

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:01.754
If you stare at that, you
can see that that is also

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.956
a repeating unit, right?

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:06.259
That is also repeating unit.

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:09.862
If I take this and
I move it down here,

00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:11.197
I get exactly the same thing.

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:15.501
And I move it over there,
and I can tile all of space.

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:19.439
I can tile all of space.

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.073
And something is
really important here.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:25.077
I can take these repeating
units and stamp them.

00:07:25.077 --> 00:07:26.813
Oh, I like that word.

00:07:26.813 --> 00:07:31.417
And I can tile all of space
and recreate this picture

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:32.985
without any voids, right?

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:34.086
I didn't miss anything.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:37.457
I filled all of space.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:38.658
I filled all of space.

00:07:38.658 --> 00:07:41.394
And that is what a
crystal system is, right?

00:07:41.394 --> 00:07:43.930
A crystal system--
let's write it here--

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.898
crystal system.

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:56.108
Crystal system-- that is
a way of enumerating--

00:07:56.108 --> 00:08:09.121
enumerates ways that
space can be filled, OK?

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:12.325
Enumerates ways that space
can be filled with no voids.

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:19.499
That's a crystal system.

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:22.835
OK, well, let's go back to
the canons for a minute,

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:26.339
because we can think about
this first in 3D before we--

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:29.876
we're just warming up
before we get to 3D, OK?

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:33.645
Oh, there it is.

00:08:33.645 --> 00:08:34.947
That's a unit cell.

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:38.618
OK, now the thing is, so
I've drawn a unit cell.

00:08:38.618 --> 00:08:40.086
And notice what I've done.

00:08:40.086 --> 00:08:45.625
I've taken this, and I've
drawn it this way, OK?

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:49.762
And those vectors define
the unit cell, right?

00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:51.731
Those vectors define
the unit cell.

00:08:51.731 --> 00:08:54.500
So those are my stamps,
the vectors there.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.435
So these things here--

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:58.404
well, I went a little
far there, but they

00:08:58.404 --> 00:09:01.407
should go to the center
of the next cannonball.

00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:02.808
So now these are vectors.

00:09:02.808 --> 00:09:06.178
If I just take these,
and I put them--

00:09:06.178 --> 00:09:08.981
now, if I translate
them over to there,

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:11.284
then I get the next unit
cell and the next one.

00:09:11.284 --> 00:09:12.952
And I go up, and I
go over, and I get

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:15.454
all the tiling of all space.

00:09:15.454 --> 00:09:17.590
And so those vectors
are really important,

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:18.491
and they have a name.

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:20.026
Those are called
the lattice vectors.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:26.566
Those are the lattice vectors.

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:33.172
I really like to think of them
as my stamps, or my stamp.

00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:34.807
It is a stamp.

00:09:34.807 --> 00:09:35.308
Mm!

00:09:35.308 --> 00:09:36.175
Mm!

00:09:36.175 --> 00:09:36.943
Mm!

00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:40.846
And no voids-- these
reactors are my stamp.

00:09:40.846 --> 00:09:42.848
Now, I'm stamping all of space.

00:09:42.848 --> 00:09:45.851
I didn't really say
what I'm putting there.

00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:47.420
I mean, here
there's cannonballs.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.555
In Escher there's ducks.

00:09:49.555 --> 00:09:51.958
Doesn't matter right now, right?

00:09:51.958 --> 00:09:53.893
It's a stamp that
fills all of space.

00:09:53.893 --> 00:10:00.032
That is how we're building
up our knowledge of crystals.

00:10:00.032 --> 00:10:04.370
OK, well you can also
ask the question,

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:08.574
which is, how much
could you fill in there?

00:10:08.574 --> 00:10:11.510
And that's a very important
question that we want--

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.947
ah-- --that we want to know.

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:18.551
OK, mm!

00:10:18.551 --> 00:10:21.554
OK, now, so I've got
my lattice vectors.

00:10:21.554 --> 00:10:23.856
But I want to know
now what am I--

00:10:23.856 --> 00:10:26.492
because I could put there--

00:10:26.492 --> 00:10:27.093
look at this.

00:10:27.093 --> 00:10:29.495
I could take a lattice
vector like this.

00:10:29.495 --> 00:10:32.131
This is a square lattice.

00:10:32.131 --> 00:10:34.300
OK, if it's a square
lattice, then those

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.402
are the same length, right?

00:10:36.402 --> 00:10:37.937
A is like the length of the--

00:10:37.937 --> 00:10:42.508
this is like the length
of the lattice vector.

00:10:46.779 --> 00:10:51.984
Now, if I took this lattice,
this stamp, and I say,

00:10:51.984 --> 00:10:54.120
OK, now I'm going to
decide what to put there--

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.055
I'm going to put circles.

00:10:56.055 --> 00:10:58.658
OK, so I'm going to put a
circle there, circle there.

00:10:58.658 --> 00:11:03.863
And what you do then is
you put a circle everywhere

00:11:03.863 --> 00:11:07.233
that you stamp, right?

00:11:07.233 --> 00:11:10.970
OK, so I have now
taken my stamp,

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:12.338
and I've stamped everywhere.

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:15.307
And I've put something
at each place--

00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:15.975
a circle.

00:11:15.975 --> 00:11:17.543
But I might ask a question.

00:11:17.543 --> 00:11:19.145
And this is a very
important question,

00:11:19.145 --> 00:11:24.750
which is, if I have
circles, what is the maximum

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:28.054
that they can pack
in for this lattice?

00:11:28.054 --> 00:11:29.889
How much can I pack?

00:11:29.889 --> 00:11:31.824
Well, you can just
visualize that now.

00:11:31.824 --> 00:11:34.193
Let's grow them.

00:11:34.193 --> 00:11:38.831
A stamp means it's the same
thing everywhere, right?

00:11:38.831 --> 00:11:41.300
So now if I make
one of them bigger,

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:44.303
well, I've made them
all bigger, right?

00:11:44.303 --> 00:11:46.706
Because they're all
the same everywhere.

00:11:46.706 --> 00:11:48.540
And if I make them
all big enough,

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:52.511
eventually they're going
to touch like that.

00:11:52.511 --> 00:11:54.847
That's a special
thing, because now I've

00:11:54.847 --> 00:11:58.017
grown the shape that I'm putting
there to the point where it's

00:11:58.017 --> 00:12:00.252
the maximum packing, right?

00:12:00.252 --> 00:12:03.255
And so you can imagine this.

00:12:03.255 --> 00:12:10.930
The area of maximum packing
is a very important parameter.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:12.932
And it's something that
we'll talk about today

00:12:12.932 --> 00:12:17.670
with 3D crystals, which
is the solids of elements

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.873
in the periodic table.

00:12:20.873 --> 00:12:24.376
But if you say with
this square analogy--

00:12:24.376 --> 00:12:25.778
so my lattice is a square.

00:12:25.778 --> 00:12:27.313
My stamp is a square.

00:12:27.313 --> 00:12:29.548
My sides are equivalent.

00:12:29.548 --> 00:12:31.016
And what I put
there is a circle.

00:12:31.016 --> 00:12:32.885
Then the area of maximum
packing is something

00:12:32.885 --> 00:12:35.921
you can calculate, right?

00:12:35.921 --> 00:12:38.224
Because the circle itself--

00:12:38.224 --> 00:12:41.026
in this case, if I--

00:12:41.026 --> 00:12:41.794
[STUDENT SNEEZES]

00:12:41.794 --> 00:12:44.130
Gezuntheit.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.798
If I look at it, then--

00:12:45.798 --> 00:12:49.168
oh, that's not a good circle.

00:12:49.168 --> 00:12:52.938
But anyway, you'll get the
point here, which is that--

00:12:52.938 --> 00:12:57.409
oh, boy-- which is that if
this is the lattice vector

00:12:57.409 --> 00:13:02.915
is a, like I drew there and I'm
looking at the maximum packing,

00:13:02.915 --> 00:13:07.386
then you know already that
the radius of the circle

00:13:07.386 --> 00:13:08.921
is a half a.

00:13:08.921 --> 00:13:13.159
So radius equals 1/2 a.

00:13:17.763 --> 00:13:22.635
If the radius of the
circle is 1/2 a--

00:13:22.635 --> 00:13:24.837
if the radius of
a circle is 1/2 a,

00:13:24.837 --> 00:13:26.839
then you also know
what the area is.

00:13:26.839 --> 00:13:28.274
I'm in 2D right now.

00:13:28.274 --> 00:13:29.842
I'm in 2D.

00:13:29.842 --> 00:13:32.311
So if I've maximized the
packing of these circles

00:13:32.311 --> 00:13:42.188
on the square lattice,
then the area of the circle

00:13:42.188 --> 00:13:49.461
is pi times r squared.

00:13:49.461 --> 00:13:57.436
So 1/2 a squared equals
pi a-squared over 4.

00:13:57.436 --> 00:14:00.506
But see, I also know how
much I packed it in, right?

00:14:00.506 --> 00:14:03.008
And I'm doing this
slowly on purpose,

00:14:03.008 --> 00:14:06.912
because we'll go faster when
we do some of the other ones.

00:14:06.912 --> 00:14:08.981
But I want to do
the first one slow,

00:14:08.981 --> 00:14:12.985
because then we'll all be
able to see what we're talking

00:14:12.985 --> 00:14:14.553
about with the simplest case.

00:14:14.553 --> 00:14:22.261
The area of the square
is a-squared, right?

00:14:22.261 --> 00:14:34.139
And so the area of max packing
is equal to pi a squared over

00:14:34.139 --> 00:14:36.675
a-squared--

00:14:36.675 --> 00:14:39.612
pi over 4.

00:14:39.612 --> 00:14:41.513
because the a-squared
is cancelled.

00:14:41.513 --> 00:14:42.314
So it's 78%.

00:14:47.786 --> 00:14:51.190
This is what Hooke was--
but did I max it out,

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:52.591
or could I do
something different?

00:14:52.591 --> 00:14:54.793
Could I pick a
different lattice?

00:14:54.793 --> 00:14:59.265
What if we made cannonballs
into rectangles?

00:14:59.265 --> 00:15:01.867
Bad idea.

00:15:01.867 --> 00:15:04.803
No, he stuck with spheres,
but how do you pack them in?

00:15:04.803 --> 00:15:09.275
How can you arrange them in a
way to change the max packing?

00:15:09.275 --> 00:15:15.114
That's what nature does
with atoms, as we'll see.

00:15:15.114 --> 00:15:19.985
So for example, if I had a 2D
hexagonal lattice, instead of

00:15:19.985 --> 00:15:22.888
my stamp being a square--

00:15:22.888 --> 00:15:30.296
so if it were a 2D
hexagonal lattice--

00:15:30.296 --> 00:15:32.798
I won't do this one out.

00:15:32.798 --> 00:15:41.941
But then the max packing
goes all the way up to 91%.

00:15:41.941 --> 00:15:44.276
That's a lot more per area--

00:15:44.276 --> 00:15:45.110
max packing.

00:15:49.815 --> 00:15:52.117
So this is something
that we care a lot about.

00:15:52.117 --> 00:15:56.355
It's something that
we care about because,

00:15:56.355 --> 00:15:59.091
when atoms see each
other in a solid, what's

00:15:59.091 --> 00:16:01.060
the first thing they
want to determine?

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.696
The first thing they want to do
is they want to go back in here

00:16:03.696 --> 00:16:08.267
and they want to be like,
which one of those can we do?

00:16:08.267 --> 00:16:12.204
Can we find some way to bond?

00:16:12.204 --> 00:16:13.706
That's what atoms do.

00:16:13.706 --> 00:16:14.974
That's what everyone does.

00:16:14.974 --> 00:16:17.776
How can we bond together?

00:16:17.776 --> 00:16:21.613
What kind of relationship
are we going to have?

00:16:21.613 --> 00:16:24.550
But they want to
max that out, right?

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:27.786
And so they want to try to pack
in, given the bond that they're

00:16:27.786 --> 00:16:29.488
going to have.

00:16:29.488 --> 00:16:31.156
And that is what's
going to dictate

00:16:31.156 --> 00:16:35.060
the kind of arrangement they
can have in the crystal, OK?

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:40.199
So OK, so here we are.

00:16:40.199 --> 00:16:42.201
And we're going from 2D to 3D.

00:16:42.201 --> 00:16:49.108
Now, the thing is,
this is not going

00:16:49.108 --> 00:16:52.177
to be an in-depth dive
into symmetry and space

00:16:52.177 --> 00:16:55.514
groups and group theory.

00:16:55.514 --> 00:16:58.417
Those are all great topics.

00:16:58.417 --> 00:17:00.452
But I'm just going to tell you--

00:17:00.452 --> 00:17:01.920
and it's actually kind of cool.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:06.458
In 3D, there are only seven
unique crystal systems.

00:17:06.458 --> 00:17:09.060
There are only seven ways
that you can do this.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:12.464
You can only pack in
and have no voids--

00:17:12.464 --> 00:17:15.800
and have no voids-- you can
only pack in these seven

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:19.104
different systems where, as you
can see, what's changing here?

00:17:19.104 --> 00:17:20.472
Well, it's Cartesian
coordinates,

00:17:20.472 --> 00:17:23.942
so you've got the length
of each lattice vector

00:17:23.942 --> 00:17:26.478
and their relative
angles, right?

00:17:26.478 --> 00:17:28.414
That's all you're changing.

00:17:28.414 --> 00:17:33.218
Well, sometimes you change
all the angles at once, OK?

00:17:33.218 --> 00:17:35.354
Sometimes you change only--

00:17:35.354 --> 00:17:37.423
well, sometimes you
change only a few of them.

00:17:37.423 --> 00:17:38.957
Sometimes you make
them all the same.

00:17:38.957 --> 00:17:40.359
Sometimes you change a few.

00:17:40.359 --> 00:17:42.895
Sometimes you have
all of them be even.

00:17:42.895 --> 00:17:45.030
And sometimes some
of them are equal,

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:46.732
some of the lattice
lengths are equal

00:17:46.732 --> 00:17:50.235
but another one is
different, all right?

00:17:50.235 --> 00:17:53.272
These are the seven ways that
we can fill all of space.

00:17:53.272 --> 00:17:55.507
And there are only seven.

00:17:55.507 --> 00:17:58.410
In this class, we're only
going to focus on one,

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:01.580
and that's the cubic system.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:05.217
And the reason is that
this gives us plenty

00:18:05.217 --> 00:18:08.654
to work with to
understand crystallography

00:18:08.654 --> 00:18:11.523
and how chemistry relates
to crystallography.

00:18:11.523 --> 00:18:16.195
And there is the benefit of
the fact that a lot of elements

00:18:16.195 --> 00:18:17.629
take one of these three--

00:18:17.629 --> 00:18:19.932
actually, only two.

00:18:19.932 --> 00:18:22.668
But they take cubic symmetry.

00:18:22.668 --> 00:18:23.602
What does that mean?

00:18:23.602 --> 00:18:30.776
Well, it means that all the
lattice vectors are the same.

00:18:30.776 --> 00:18:33.445
Each one has a length a.

00:18:33.445 --> 00:18:36.815
They're orthogonal to
each other, which means

00:18:36.815 --> 00:18:39.184
that the angle is 90 degrees.

00:18:39.184 --> 00:18:40.953
So we've got a cube.

00:18:40.953 --> 00:18:45.023
Now, it turns out that now we
say, well, how do you pack?

00:18:45.023 --> 00:18:51.563
How can I pack things
together in the cubic system?

00:18:51.563 --> 00:18:55.567
And there's three
different ways to do it.

00:18:55.567 --> 00:19:01.073
So what this is is it's more
symmetry and more group theory

00:19:01.073 --> 00:19:05.611
and math that goes into this
that Bravais figured out.

00:19:05.611 --> 00:19:09.047
And so we call these
Bravais lattices.

00:19:09.047 --> 00:19:10.849
Now, what Bravais figured out--

00:19:10.849 --> 00:19:13.318
and again, you don't need to
know all the math behind this,

00:19:13.318 --> 00:19:17.956
but I want you to know
these three cubic lattices.

00:19:17.956 --> 00:19:21.493
What you figured out is that
from the seven crystal systems,

00:19:21.493 --> 00:19:24.696
there are only 14 ways to pack.

00:19:24.696 --> 00:19:26.431
There are 14 ways to pack.

00:19:26.431 --> 00:19:28.700
That's it, no more.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:29.635
That's pretty cool.

00:19:29.635 --> 00:19:31.803
There's no 15.

00:19:31.803 --> 00:19:33.005
There's no 16.

00:19:33.005 --> 00:19:34.706
There's 14 exactly--

00:19:34.706 --> 00:19:39.511
14 ways that you can, by having
a lattice, which means a stamp,

00:19:39.511 --> 00:19:41.346
there are only 14
ways you can do it

00:19:41.346 --> 00:19:42.581
for the seven crystal systems.

00:19:42.581 --> 00:19:45.651
And for the cubic system,
there's only three.

00:19:45.651 --> 00:19:48.887
So I have three types of stamp.

00:19:48.887 --> 00:19:51.790
That's what it means,
three types of stamps

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:54.793
where I leave no voids, right?

00:19:54.793 --> 00:19:55.494
I leave no voids.

00:19:55.494 --> 00:19:58.630
So I can tile all
of 3D space, right?

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:05.504
OK, and it forms a cubic
unit cell, cubic unit cell.

00:20:05.504 --> 00:20:06.805
These are the three ways--

00:20:06.805 --> 00:20:10.342
a simple cubic, a
body-centered cubic,

00:20:10.342 --> 00:20:11.610
and a face-centered cubic.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:14.513
These are the three
types of lattices

00:20:14.513 --> 00:20:15.914
that have cubic symmetry.

00:20:15.914 --> 00:20:17.482
They are the only three.

00:20:17.482 --> 00:20:21.653
So in a Bravais
lattice, the difference

00:20:21.653 --> 00:20:27.859
is in a Bravais lattice what
Bravais did was he enumerated--

00:20:27.859 --> 00:20:30.128
so here we enumerate the
ways space can be filled.

00:20:32.965 --> 00:20:42.774
He enumerated the
packing, all the ways

00:20:42.774 --> 00:20:46.745
that the space can
be packed with,

00:20:46.745 --> 00:20:50.949
for example, packing
within the unit cell.

00:20:54.553 --> 00:20:59.424
So there's three distinct ways
to pack within a cubic unit

00:20:59.424 --> 00:21:01.093
cell, OK?

00:21:01.093 --> 00:21:03.595
That's what we're going
to bring to life today

00:21:03.595 --> 00:21:06.164
and we'll talk more about
on Wednesday as well.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:13.305
And what I want to do is I
want to go through each one.

00:21:13.305 --> 00:21:15.040
So we're going to go
through simple cubic,

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:17.409
body-centered cubic,
face-centered cubic.

00:21:17.409 --> 00:21:21.380
And then with each one,
I'm going to talk about it,

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:23.382
and then I'm going to
show you a little video so

00:21:23.382 --> 00:21:26.652
that we can really see how the
unit cell gets kind of sliced

00:21:26.652 --> 00:21:27.152
up.

00:21:27.152 --> 00:21:29.655
And there's like a razor
that comes in and slices it.

00:21:29.655 --> 00:21:30.589
It's really cool.

00:21:30.589 --> 00:21:34.526
And so you can really see and
feel what we're talking about.

00:21:34.526 --> 00:21:37.362
And speaking of seeing and
feeling, so I have these.

00:21:37.362 --> 00:21:42.100
And I had wanted for these to
be what go in your goody bag.

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:46.672
But apparently like $100,000
is too much or whatever.

00:21:46.672 --> 00:21:48.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:51.176
But so you have smaller
versions of these.

00:21:51.176 --> 00:21:52.411
And I encourage you--

00:21:52.411 --> 00:21:55.614
there's no better way to
think about crystallography

00:21:55.614 --> 00:21:58.250
than to build stuff and see it.

00:21:58.250 --> 00:21:59.151
You've got to do that.

00:21:59.151 --> 00:22:00.252
It's really cool.

00:22:00.252 --> 00:22:03.922
And so this is the simple cubic.

00:22:03.922 --> 00:22:05.457
OK, so I'll pass that around.

00:22:05.457 --> 00:22:08.026
And this is the
body-centered cubic.

00:22:08.026 --> 00:22:11.063
And here you have
spheres, right?

00:22:11.063 --> 00:22:11.897
That's fine.

00:22:11.897 --> 00:22:16.201
But all we've done so
far is set up a lattice.

00:22:16.201 --> 00:22:17.436
This could be anything.

00:22:17.436 --> 00:22:20.372
It's a repeat lattice.

00:22:20.372 --> 00:22:21.673
Well, that's what a lattice is.

00:22:21.673 --> 00:22:25.977
The lattice just defines that
this is the same as this.

00:22:25.977 --> 00:22:29.648
Whatever is here is the
same, is the same as this,

00:22:29.648 --> 00:22:30.716
is the same as this.

00:22:30.716 --> 00:22:34.186
That's what we're
defining here, OK?

00:22:34.186 --> 00:22:42.661
OK, all right, now let's
talk about these three.

00:22:42.661 --> 00:22:45.163
This is a picture
from [? April. ?]

00:22:45.163 --> 00:22:47.466
These are the three systems
I just talked about,

00:22:47.466 --> 00:22:49.768
the three Bravais lattices,
three different ways

00:22:49.768 --> 00:22:55.707
to pack in a cubic
symmetric cell.

00:22:55.707 --> 00:22:57.976
And notice there's three
ways of looking at it, right?

00:22:57.976 --> 00:23:01.113
So here is sort of a
ball-and-stick model,

00:23:01.113 --> 00:23:04.683
which makes it easy to see
the bonding and the atoms.

00:23:04.683 --> 00:23:07.586
But the space-filling model
is really important here,

00:23:07.586 --> 00:23:09.821
because the space-filling
model lets us think

00:23:09.821 --> 00:23:12.591
about the maximum packing.

00:23:12.591 --> 00:23:13.592
That's really important.

00:23:13.592 --> 00:23:15.827
Remember the maximum
packing, because remember

00:23:15.827 --> 00:23:17.963
these are bonding.

00:23:17.963 --> 00:23:19.398
Really?

00:23:19.398 --> 00:23:22.768
These are bonding
together, right?

00:23:22.768 --> 00:23:26.304
So drawing them all the
way up to where they touch,

00:23:26.304 --> 00:23:29.441
that's going to help
us understand what

00:23:29.441 --> 00:23:30.809
their maximum packing can be.

00:23:30.809 --> 00:23:33.111
So let's look at that
for the simple cubic, OK?

00:23:33.111 --> 00:23:36.014
So for the simple cubic,
we'll do that here--

00:23:36.014 --> 00:23:36.848
simple cubic.

00:23:39.918 --> 00:23:46.825
OK, simple cubic-- oh, let's
just call it SC for short.

00:23:46.825 --> 00:23:47.793
Why not?

00:23:47.793 --> 00:23:49.728
Now, one of the things
you want to think about

00:23:49.728 --> 00:23:52.130
is, how many nearest
neighbors do I have?

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:53.064
And I lost the--

00:23:53.064 --> 00:23:54.032
so you can see.

00:23:54.032 --> 00:23:55.600
Look at look at
the edge and think

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:57.402
about how many nearest
neighbors-- well, you

00:23:57.402 --> 00:23:58.770
know you got six, right?

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:02.240
If you're in the corner there of
the cube in your simple cubic,

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:03.642
you've got six
nearest neighbors.

00:24:03.642 --> 00:24:05.243
OK, that's important, right?

00:24:05.243 --> 00:24:13.385
Six nearest neighbors--
why is it important?

00:24:13.385 --> 00:24:17.255
Well, it's important
because that's

00:24:17.255 --> 00:24:25.130
how many other atoms you could
bond with in this solid, OK?

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:28.700
But the other thing we want
to know is the packing.

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:34.272
Now, again, what you
want to do to figure out

00:24:34.272 --> 00:24:37.709
the maximum packing is
you want to think about it

00:24:37.709 --> 00:24:39.044
in the same way we did here.

00:24:39.044 --> 00:24:43.415
Think about putting something
at each of the lattice points

00:24:43.415 --> 00:24:46.251
and then growing
it out until they

00:24:46.251 --> 00:24:47.686
can't grow any more, right?

00:24:47.686 --> 00:24:49.754
And that's a good way
to think about it.

00:24:49.754 --> 00:24:52.224
And in this case, we're
going to take spheres.

00:24:52.224 --> 00:24:54.626
We're going to be taking
spheres because that's how

00:24:54.626 --> 00:24:56.161
we're going to represent atoms.

00:24:56.161 --> 00:24:57.896
So we're going to
be taking spheres.

00:24:57.896 --> 00:25:01.366
And we're going to grow them
out and see what the packing is.

00:25:01.366 --> 00:25:05.370
Yeah, but I've got to do
the same trick I did here.

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:07.105
I've got to do the
same trick I did here.

00:25:07.105 --> 00:25:11.076
I've got to write the radius of
the atom in terms of the unit

00:25:11.076 --> 00:25:12.210
cell.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:15.313
So if the unit cell--

00:25:15.313 --> 00:25:16.448
oh, boy.

00:25:16.448 --> 00:25:19.451
I'm going to be
drawing a 3D structure.

00:25:19.451 --> 00:25:20.218
Danger!

00:25:20.218 --> 00:25:25.490
a, a, a-- that's a cube.

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:27.826
All the sides are a.

00:25:27.826 --> 00:25:31.062
I made myself very happy.

00:25:31.062 --> 00:25:31.897
It's a cube.

00:25:31.897 --> 00:25:34.499
Now-- oh, but we have a cube.

00:25:34.499 --> 00:25:36.601
Now, OK, here we go.

00:25:36.601 --> 00:25:40.171
This one-- oh, but see,
I want maximum packing.

00:25:40.171 --> 00:25:42.541
So I'm going to grow them out.

00:25:42.541 --> 00:25:48.346
And you can see what's going
to happen as I do that.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:55.153
And so you can see that in the
case of a simple cubic lattice,

00:25:55.153 --> 00:25:57.055
it's just like in
the square lattice.

00:25:57.055 --> 00:25:59.491
It's just like in the
square lattice, right?

00:25:59.491 --> 00:26:04.362
When I've grown out the sphere,
the radius of that sphere,

00:26:04.362 --> 00:26:09.501
the radius is r equals--

00:26:09.501 --> 00:26:10.802
now, oh, wait a second.

00:26:10.802 --> 00:26:13.738
OK, so I have 4/3 pi r--

00:26:13.738 --> 00:26:15.507
OK, but that doesn't matter yet.

00:26:15.507 --> 00:26:16.408
Don't do volume.

00:26:16.408 --> 00:26:17.909
Think about the radius

00:26:17.909 --> 00:26:21.279
OK, the radius is just going
to be 1/2 the lattice, right?

00:26:21.279 --> 00:26:23.248
So radius is 1/2 a.

00:26:23.248 --> 00:26:30.155
Volume is 4/3 pi 1/2 a cubed.

00:26:33.058 --> 00:26:34.392
Now, this is important.

00:26:34.392 --> 00:26:42.767
That is at the max packing.

00:26:42.767 --> 00:26:46.037
OK, so I've grown
these volumes out,

00:26:46.037 --> 00:26:48.106
and I've seen what
the packing can be.

00:26:48.106 --> 00:26:50.075
Now, we actually call this--

00:26:50.075 --> 00:26:53.378
so now I've got the
volume of an atom.

00:26:53.378 --> 00:26:55.513
I'm pretending that
these are atoms now.

00:26:55.513 --> 00:26:57.315
No, I'm not pretending anymore.

00:26:57.315 --> 00:26:58.984
It's real.

00:26:58.984 --> 00:27:01.953
These spheres are now atoms.

00:27:01.953 --> 00:27:06.891
And OK, so that's the
volume of the atom, right?

00:27:06.891 --> 00:27:19.871
And so we can write
atomic packing fraction

00:27:19.871 --> 00:27:26.044
as the volume of the atom
over the volume of the cell.

00:27:26.044 --> 00:27:29.214
That's exactly what
I did up here, right?

00:27:29.214 --> 00:27:33.118
That's how we got the
area of the square,

00:27:33.118 --> 00:27:36.655
the area of the circle, right?

00:27:36.655 --> 00:27:39.324
But there's one more thing
that's really important,

00:27:39.324 --> 00:27:47.399
which is it has to be times the
number of atoms in the cell.

00:27:47.399 --> 00:27:50.301
When I say cell,
I mean unit cell.

00:27:50.301 --> 00:27:52.704
It has to be times the number
of atoms in the unit cell.

00:27:55.807 --> 00:27:58.043
So it's however
many atoms I have

00:27:58.043 --> 00:28:00.045
times the volume of the atom.

00:28:00.045 --> 00:28:01.046
OK, that's good.

00:28:01.046 --> 00:28:03.148
That's how much is
inside of the cell

00:28:03.148 --> 00:28:04.749
divided by the
volume of the cell,

00:28:04.749 --> 00:28:08.386
and that gives me the
atomic packing fraction.

00:28:08.386 --> 00:28:12.424
So in the case of
a simple cubic,

00:28:12.424 --> 00:28:16.928
the APF, the Atomic
Packing Fraction,

00:28:16.928 --> 00:28:25.470
is equal to pi over
6, which is 52%.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:29.974
That's not very good, all right?

00:28:29.974 --> 00:28:32.944
That's not-- well, I
mean, it's relative.

00:28:32.944 --> 00:28:33.678
We don't know yet.

00:28:33.678 --> 00:28:35.413
But it feels kind of low--

00:28:35.413 --> 00:28:36.214
52%.

00:28:36.214 --> 00:28:39.117
So with a simple
cubic lattice, all

00:28:39.117 --> 00:28:45.423
I can do in my wildest dreams
is cover 52% of that volume.

00:28:45.423 --> 00:28:49.094
That's not very-- that
doesn't feel like a lot.

00:28:49.094 --> 00:28:53.398
And in fact, when you look
at the periodic table, what

00:28:53.398 --> 00:28:56.201
you find is that there's
only one element--

00:28:56.201 --> 00:28:58.903
one-- that takes the
simple cubic lattice.

00:28:58.903 --> 00:29:01.239
Anybody know what it is?

00:29:01.239 --> 00:29:03.541
Of course-- it's polonium.

00:29:03.541 --> 00:29:04.876
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:04.876 --> 00:29:06.544
Of course.

00:29:06.544 --> 00:29:09.180
That's a really good
conversation starter.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:11.282
I'm just saying.

00:29:11.282 --> 00:29:14.018
That is a great
way to meet people.

00:29:14.018 --> 00:29:14.619
Which one?

00:29:14.619 --> 00:29:16.354
It's polonium?

00:29:16.354 --> 00:29:17.055
Why?

00:29:17.055 --> 00:29:19.691
Relativity.

00:29:19.691 --> 00:29:24.462
In polonium, those electrons
are so high energy--

00:29:24.462 --> 00:29:25.029
literally.

00:29:25.029 --> 00:29:26.631
They're so high energy
they're like close

00:29:26.631 --> 00:29:27.565
to the speed of light.

00:29:27.565 --> 00:29:28.733
They're relativistic.

00:29:28.733 --> 00:29:29.801
Their mass is heavier.

00:29:29.801 --> 00:29:32.570
All sorts of interesting
things happen.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:36.407
Polonium is the only one
that goes simple cubic.

00:29:36.407 --> 00:29:37.509
I promised a video.

00:29:37.509 --> 00:29:39.043
Oh, there's the-- oh.

00:29:39.043 --> 00:29:40.912
Ha.

00:29:40.912 --> 00:29:43.982
a-- a-- a.

00:29:43.982 --> 00:29:46.417
There is a diagonal.

00:29:46.417 --> 00:29:47.786
There is another diagonal.

00:29:47.786 --> 00:29:48.353
Oh!

00:29:48.353 --> 00:29:51.956
We're going to
come back to this.

00:29:51.956 --> 00:29:53.191
Let's draw that.

00:29:53.191 --> 00:29:55.126
Because that might
be important, right?

00:29:55.126 --> 00:29:59.097
So if I have a cube,
then this diagonal,

00:29:59.097 --> 00:30:04.269
if that's a and that's
a, then that is a root 2.

00:30:04.269 --> 00:30:06.538
And the body diagonal--

00:30:06.538 --> 00:30:07.906
that's the body diagonal--

00:30:11.242 --> 00:30:14.979
is a root 3--

00:30:14.979 --> 00:30:18.116
equals a root 3, right?

00:30:18.116 --> 00:30:21.419
OK, we'll come back--

00:30:21.419 --> 00:30:24.756
oh, I didn't need that,
because this is just a over 2.

00:30:24.756 --> 00:30:27.225
But whoa, hold on.

00:30:27.225 --> 00:30:28.026
We're coming to it.

00:30:28.026 --> 00:30:29.561
But I promised a video first.

00:30:29.561 --> 00:30:32.530
So here's a video with
the laser coming in.

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:34.065
And you're going to
really see-- this

00:30:34.065 --> 00:30:35.266
will come to life a little bit.

00:30:35.266 --> 00:30:35.934
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:30:35.934 --> 00:30:38.736
- [INAUDIBLE] of a cube.

00:30:38.736 --> 00:30:41.606
The simple, or primitive
cubic unit cell

00:30:41.606 --> 00:30:44.876
has particles at
the corners only.

00:30:44.876 --> 00:30:47.812
In reality, the
particles lie as close

00:30:47.812 --> 00:30:50.148
to each other as possible.

00:30:50.148 --> 00:30:53.651
Note that the particles
touch along the cube edges

00:30:53.651 --> 00:30:58.056
but not along a diagonal in
the face or along a diagonal

00:30:58.056 --> 00:30:59.924
through the body.

00:30:59.924 --> 00:31:03.294
By slicing away parts that
belong to neighboring unit

00:31:03.294 --> 00:31:06.831
cells, we see that the
actual unit cell consists

00:31:06.831 --> 00:31:10.368
of portions of the particles.

00:31:10.368 --> 00:31:14.038
When the cells pack next to each
other in all three dimensions,

00:31:14.038 --> 00:31:16.574
we obtain the crystal.

00:31:16.574 --> 00:31:20.311
If we fade the others out,
you can see the original group

00:31:20.311 --> 00:31:23.381
of eight particles within
the array and the unit cell

00:31:23.381 --> 00:31:24.949
within that group.

00:31:24.949 --> 00:31:29.454
We find the number of particles
in one unit cell by combining

00:31:29.454 --> 00:31:32.190
all the particles' portions.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:36.194
In the simple cubic unit cell,
eight corners, each of which

00:31:36.194 --> 00:31:41.599
is 1/8 of a particle,
combine to give one particle.

00:31:41.599 --> 00:31:43.735
A key feature of a
crystal structure

00:31:43.735 --> 00:31:47.572
is its coordination number, the
number of the nearest neighbors

00:31:47.572 --> 00:31:49.874
surrounding each particle.

00:31:49.874 --> 00:31:52.944
In a simple cubic array,
any given particle

00:31:52.944 --> 00:31:56.581
has a neighboring particle
above, below, to the right,

00:31:56.581 --> 00:31:59.250
to the left, in front,
and in back of it,

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:03.154
for a total of six
nearest neighbors.

00:32:03.154 --> 00:32:05.723
The body-centered
cubic unit cell has a--

00:32:05.723 --> 00:32:07.759
[END PLAYBACK]

00:32:07.759 --> 00:32:10.628
OK, so you see-- that
was so cool, right?

00:32:10.628 --> 00:32:13.064
Like unh, unh, and you're
figuring out how many

00:32:13.064 --> 00:32:17.735
atoms are in the cell, right?

00:32:17.735 --> 00:32:20.004
Well, why didn't you just
put the atom in the middle?

00:32:20.004 --> 00:32:24.309
You could do that, but then
it's not at the point, right?

00:32:24.309 --> 00:32:27.312
So you've got to understand,
the concept of the lattice

00:32:27.312 --> 00:32:30.982
is that whatever I put here,
wherever my vectors go,

00:32:30.982 --> 00:32:35.186
that's what I put there
and there and there.

00:32:35.186 --> 00:32:37.689
And so we often as a
standard, we say, well, OK,

00:32:37.689 --> 00:32:38.556
what did I put there?

00:32:38.556 --> 00:32:41.926
I put an atom and then
I put another atom.

00:32:41.926 --> 00:32:44.696
But then your unit
cell is that cube.

00:32:44.696 --> 00:32:46.831
And so we cut the unit
cell, and you're like, well,

00:32:46.831 --> 00:32:48.499
how many atoms do I have in it?

00:32:48.499 --> 00:32:49.167
Well, let's see.

00:32:49.167 --> 00:32:53.004
I got one here, but it's shared
with all these other cubes,

00:32:53.004 --> 00:32:55.740
so there's an 1/8 of an atom
there and 1/8 of an atom there.

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:58.109
We add them all up, and you
get one atom right back,

00:32:58.109 --> 00:32:59.911
which you knew--

00:32:59.911 --> 00:33:00.912
which you knew.

00:33:00.912 --> 00:33:04.549
And by the way, this
was not 1 over a.

00:33:04.549 --> 00:33:05.350
It was.

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:07.885
I wrote it mistakenly 1 over a.

00:33:07.885 --> 00:33:13.257
The radius is 1/2 a over
there, which is correct here.

00:33:13.257 --> 00:33:14.192
OK, good.

00:33:14.192 --> 00:33:17.895
Now, next crystal
structure-- oh, look.

00:33:17.895 --> 00:33:22.467
How do you know what
crystal structure--

00:33:22.467 --> 00:33:24.969
there it is right there!

00:33:24.969 --> 00:33:27.972
It's the periodic table!

00:33:27.972 --> 00:33:29.907
To the rescue again--
he had it right there.

00:33:29.907 --> 00:33:30.408
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:30.408 --> 00:33:30.942
Thank you.

00:33:30.942 --> 00:33:33.311
[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]

00:33:33.311 --> 00:33:42.053
That means T-shirts and T-shirts
and T-shirts and T-shirts.

00:33:42.053 --> 00:33:43.755
[CHATTER]

00:33:43.755 --> 00:33:49.260
All right, I'm going all the
way up there and up there.

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:50.628
We'll bring more.

00:33:50.628 --> 00:33:53.264
You bring a periodic
table like that--

00:33:53.264 --> 00:33:54.499
what is a crystal symmetry?

00:33:57.902 --> 00:33:59.971
I know.

00:33:59.971 --> 00:34:02.607
Which one is simple cubic?

00:34:02.607 --> 00:34:03.908
Polonium.

00:34:03.908 --> 00:34:05.610
Where's-- it's only polonium.

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:06.778
Oh, there it is--

00:34:06.778 --> 00:34:07.879
polonium.

00:34:07.879 --> 00:34:09.414
Thank you for being
representative

00:34:09.414 --> 00:34:11.315
of simple cubic crystals.

00:34:11.315 --> 00:34:13.351
But now we're talking
about the next kind,

00:34:13.351 --> 00:34:15.987
which is body-centered cubic.

00:34:15.987 --> 00:34:19.857
And look, a lot more have
a body-centered cubic.

00:34:19.857 --> 00:34:28.299
And here, OK, so if it's
BCC, Body-Centered Cubic,

00:34:28.299 --> 00:34:35.239
well, now you know if I take
these atoms at these corners,

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:37.442
and I add now--
there's a lattice

00:34:37.442 --> 00:34:39.911
that takes me to the middle.

00:34:39.911 --> 00:34:44.181
Remember, my lattice is
giving me this symmetry.

00:34:44.181 --> 00:34:45.449
My lattice is giving me this.

00:34:45.449 --> 00:34:49.187
So it means whatever I put
here, I put in all the corners

00:34:49.187 --> 00:34:51.755
and I put in the middle, right?

00:34:51.755 --> 00:34:52.790
That's BCC.

00:34:52.790 --> 00:34:55.426
That's what BCC means.

00:34:55.426 --> 00:34:57.428
Well, and yeah,
if you grow that--

00:34:57.428 --> 00:35:00.331
OK, so first of all,
eight nearest neighbors--

00:35:03.267 --> 00:35:04.802
eight nearest
neighbors-- well, you

00:35:04.802 --> 00:35:07.405
can see that, because
I've got the six,

00:35:07.405 --> 00:35:09.707
but then I've got
these ones here, right?

00:35:09.707 --> 00:35:13.744
I've got actually
it's not the six.

00:35:13.744 --> 00:35:15.813
I've got the one
along the diagonal,

00:35:15.813 --> 00:35:18.716
which is kind of the point
I wanted to make next.

00:35:18.716 --> 00:35:20.384
Oh, this is BCC.

00:35:20.384 --> 00:35:23.554
This is BCC, right?

00:35:23.554 --> 00:35:27.258
Now, how do you
see that it's BCC?

00:35:27.258 --> 00:35:32.130
Because you stare at it and you
stare at it and you build it.

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:34.565
You get together with friends.

00:35:34.565 --> 00:35:37.502
You get together with friends,
and you build even more.

00:35:37.502 --> 00:35:40.104
And you see how much you can
build, and then you look,

00:35:40.104 --> 00:35:41.706
and you see, look here it is.

00:35:41.706 --> 00:35:43.908
There is my square, right?

00:35:43.908 --> 00:35:45.209
OK, and there's another square.

00:35:45.209 --> 00:35:46.811
And that's my cube.

00:35:46.811 --> 00:35:48.479
And you go like this.

00:35:48.479 --> 00:35:50.281
And then you're like,
oh, look, and there's

00:35:50.281 --> 00:35:51.849
an atom in the middle.

00:35:51.849 --> 00:35:52.683
That's BCC.

00:35:52.683 --> 00:35:53.851
And they're all the same.

00:35:53.851 --> 00:35:55.553
That's BCC, right?

00:35:55.553 --> 00:35:58.956
But see, because this is
defined by the lattice,

00:35:58.956 --> 00:36:01.392
I could have made
my square anywhere.

00:36:01.392 --> 00:36:02.994
This didn't have to
be the middle atom.

00:36:02.994 --> 00:36:04.896
I could move it
over, and then this

00:36:04.896 --> 00:36:08.366
would be the middle
atom or this, right?

00:36:08.366 --> 00:36:09.467
They're all equivalent.

00:36:09.467 --> 00:36:11.235
It's a lattice.

00:36:11.235 --> 00:36:18.609
Now, if you look at this and you
take that square or that cube,

00:36:18.609 --> 00:36:21.345
then what you'll find
is that if you go along

00:36:21.345 --> 00:36:24.115
the body diagonal-- see, now
I'm going to take each of these,

00:36:24.115 --> 00:36:26.250
and I'm going to grow them.

00:36:26.250 --> 00:36:28.886
Where do the atoms touch?

00:36:28.886 --> 00:36:31.355
If I grow the atoms
in a BCC crystal,

00:36:31.355 --> 00:36:35.860
they touch along the
body diagonal first.

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:37.094
Now, that's really important.

00:36:37.094 --> 00:36:38.763
Let's pass this one around, too.

00:36:38.763 --> 00:36:42.700
That's really important, OK?

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:51.242
Because now we have this concept
that is also very important,

00:36:51.242 --> 00:36:53.144
which is the
close-packed direction--

00:36:56.447 --> 00:37:02.453
close-packed direction.

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:09.627
And for the BCC structure,
it's the body diagonal.

00:37:15.266 --> 00:37:17.768
Over here it's the cube edge.

00:37:17.768 --> 00:37:21.105
For a simple cubic,
it's the cube edge.

00:37:21.105 --> 00:37:25.776
Cube edge is the close-packed--

00:37:25.776 --> 00:37:27.278
OK, how is that going to work?

00:37:27.278 --> 00:37:28.145
I'll do this.

00:37:30.781 --> 00:37:33.317
No, that's going
to be confusing.

00:37:33.317 --> 00:37:39.590
Cube edge close-packed--
oh, that's really small.

00:37:39.590 --> 00:37:44.528
But you get my point,
close-packed direction.

00:37:44.528 --> 00:37:46.764
Close-packed direction,
body diagonal--

00:37:46.764 --> 00:37:48.332
what that means is that--

00:37:48.332 --> 00:37:52.069
and you can see it right here.

00:37:52.069 --> 00:37:56.007
If you go all the way back
to this-- there it is.

00:37:56.007 --> 00:37:57.975
As I grow these--
these are the same.

00:37:57.975 --> 00:38:01.245
They're colored blue just for
clarity, just you can see.

00:38:01.245 --> 00:38:04.282
But these are the same
because it's a BCC lattice.

00:38:04.282 --> 00:38:06.851
And as I grow them, these
all have the same radius.

00:38:06.851 --> 00:38:09.687
These are the ones that touch
first along the diagonal, not

00:38:09.687 --> 00:38:11.555
those, all right?

00:38:11.555 --> 00:38:13.357
Not those.

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:16.894
And that feeds into what
the radius is of the atom.

00:38:16.894 --> 00:38:27.138
I do the same trick, and the
APF is going to be 4/3 pi.

00:38:27.138 --> 00:38:32.310
And now I've got
my a root 3 over 4.

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:32.910
Why?

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:36.847
Because a is the
length of the cube.

00:38:36.847 --> 00:38:40.651
And you can see here I've
got four radii, right?

00:38:40.651 --> 00:38:45.456
Two diameters of the sphere
go down the body diagonal.

00:38:45.456 --> 00:38:49.293
So r cubed, 4/3 pi
r cubed, is going

00:38:49.293 --> 00:38:54.598
to be the volume of a
maximally-packed sphere

00:38:54.598 --> 00:38:57.501
in a BCC lattice.

00:38:57.501 --> 00:39:03.040
So this has to be divided by the
whole volume, which is a-cubed.

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:08.045
But I'm missing something,
because there's not just one.

00:39:08.045 --> 00:39:08.946
There's not just one.

00:39:08.946 --> 00:39:10.448
There's another one.

00:39:10.448 --> 00:39:13.684
There's actually two
atoms in a unit cell.

00:39:13.684 --> 00:39:14.552
There's two of these.

00:39:14.552 --> 00:39:15.653
You can see that
one in the middle

00:39:15.653 --> 00:39:17.621
and then that eighth,
eighth, eighth, eighth.

00:39:17.621 --> 00:39:18.522
You've got two.

00:39:18.522 --> 00:39:25.162
So I've got to multiply it
by 2, and it gives you 68%,

00:39:25.162 --> 00:39:26.764
is the APF.

00:39:26.764 --> 00:39:31.302
And that's why so many more
structures, especially metals,

00:39:31.302 --> 00:39:33.170
are happy to be BCC.

00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:36.107
Let's watch the video
again, or a different video,

00:39:36.107 --> 00:39:36.874
this one on BBC.

00:39:36.874 --> 00:39:37.541
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:39:37.541 --> 00:39:40.644
- --particle at each corner
and one in the center,

00:39:40.644 --> 00:39:44.148
which is colored pink to
make it easier to see.

00:39:44.148 --> 00:39:46.550
With full-size
spheres, you can see

00:39:46.550 --> 00:39:49.320
that the particles don't touch
along the edges of the cube.

00:39:49.320 --> 00:39:49.887
Close-packing

00:39:49.887 --> 00:39:54.992
- But each corner particle does
touch the one in the center.

00:39:54.992 --> 00:39:58.195
The actual unit cell
consists of portions

00:39:58.195 --> 00:40:02.199
of the corner particles and
the whole one in the center.

00:40:02.199 --> 00:40:04.935
Eight eighths give one particle.

00:40:04.935 --> 00:40:07.037
And the one in the
center gives another,

00:40:07.037 --> 00:40:10.274
for a total of two particles.

00:40:10.274 --> 00:40:13.944
In this tiny portion of a
body-centered cubic array,

00:40:13.944 --> 00:40:16.213
you can see that any
given particle has

00:40:16.213 --> 00:40:19.350
four nearest neighbors
above and four

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:23.621
below, for a total of
eight nearest neighbors.

00:40:23.621 --> 00:40:24.221
[END PLAYBACK]

00:40:24.221 --> 00:40:26.323
OK, now it's really
important, right?

00:40:26.323 --> 00:40:28.893
Just so you see conceptually,
visually how we got this,

00:40:28.893 --> 00:40:33.497
the close-packed direction for
BCC is four times the radius.

00:40:33.497 --> 00:40:38.135
I want to write that,
four times the radius.

00:40:38.135 --> 00:40:40.438
And it's that thing
that we're doing now

00:40:40.438 --> 00:40:43.941
a couple of times, where you've
got the radius of this sphere

00:40:43.941 --> 00:40:46.977
that you're packing in
with a given symmetry.

00:40:46.977 --> 00:40:49.113
But I've got to
write that in terms

00:40:49.113 --> 00:40:52.950
of the lattice vectors, which
are in this case all a, right?

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:53.918
So it's really easy.

00:40:53.918 --> 00:40:56.053
But they're all a, so I've
got to write it in terms

00:40:56.053 --> 00:40:57.254
of the length of the lattice.

00:40:57.254 --> 00:41:01.459
That's how you get the
atomic packing, OK?

00:41:01.459 --> 00:41:04.295
Good, now the last one is FCC.

00:41:04.295 --> 00:41:06.897
Look at how many elements
take the FCC form.

00:41:06.897 --> 00:41:07.665
How do you know?

00:41:07.665 --> 00:41:11.068
You bring out the periodic
table, and you look it up.

00:41:11.068 --> 00:41:15.473
Now, many of these elements
can take different crystal

00:41:15.473 --> 00:41:16.674
symmetries.

00:41:16.674 --> 00:41:19.610
The ones that are listed are
the ones that are often--

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:22.146
well, always the most
stable one, right.

00:41:22.146 --> 00:41:23.314
They're the most stable one.

00:41:23.314 --> 00:41:26.484
But sometimes they can take
many different symmetries

00:41:26.484 --> 00:41:30.321
if you do something like
add temperature or pressure

00:41:30.321 --> 00:41:32.456
or other things to the system.

00:41:32.456 --> 00:41:34.859
What's in the periodic table
is the most stable one.

00:41:34.859 --> 00:41:42.099
Now, the FCC is the last one
that we're going to care about.

00:41:42.099 --> 00:41:47.338
And it is the
face-centered cubic.

00:41:47.338 --> 00:41:54.812
Now, OK, so on this one,
this is the face, right?

00:41:54.812 --> 00:41:59.316
So we've got these atoms that
are sharing across the face.

00:41:59.316 --> 00:42:03.320
And then we've got these
atoms here at the corners.

00:42:03.320 --> 00:42:07.725
There's nothing in the middle
now, but they're on the faces--

00:42:07.725 --> 00:42:11.328
sorry, the corners and
the faces, all right?

00:42:11.328 --> 00:42:15.799
And so if you look
at this, what you

00:42:15.799 --> 00:42:19.703
can see from looking at this--
and you've got to look at it.

00:42:19.703 --> 00:42:21.305
Where's my cube?

00:42:21.305 --> 00:42:22.573
Where's my unit cell?

00:42:22.573 --> 00:42:25.609
You now know it's called
the unit cell, right?

00:42:25.609 --> 00:42:26.477
Oh, and I see.

00:42:26.477 --> 00:42:28.312
And then there's the
face atom, right?

00:42:28.312 --> 00:42:30.414
There's a square there.

00:42:30.414 --> 00:42:32.683
Then there's the
atom in the face.

00:42:32.683 --> 00:42:34.418
And then there's
nothing in the middle.

00:42:34.418 --> 00:42:35.986
It's not BCC, right?

00:42:35.986 --> 00:42:40.057
But if I look at
another edge of that,

00:42:40.057 --> 00:42:43.994
there's the atom in that face
and so on and so on, right?

00:42:43.994 --> 00:42:47.197
Now, what's really
cool about FCC

00:42:47.197 --> 00:42:49.900
is that it's got a
lot of neighbors.

00:42:49.900 --> 00:42:52.369
And it's got more packing.

00:42:52.369 --> 00:42:58.742
So for FCC, this is the
close-packed direction.

00:42:58.742 --> 00:43:07.418
Close-packed
direction is the face,

00:43:07.418 --> 00:43:09.587
is the-- did I write down?

00:43:09.587 --> 00:43:11.422
I didn't write down.

00:43:11.422 --> 00:43:13.057
OK, that's fine.

00:43:13.057 --> 00:43:23.000
It's the face diagonal,
which is a root 2 in length.

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:27.171
And so the nearest
neighbors is 12.

00:43:27.171 --> 00:43:35.479
And the atomic packing fraction
for FCC is 4/3 pi times--

00:43:35.479 --> 00:43:37.881
and now here we go, right?

00:43:37.881 --> 00:43:41.085
When I expand them,
it's along the face

00:43:41.085 --> 00:43:43.354
that they're going
to touch, right?

00:43:43.354 --> 00:43:45.356
So that's the
close-packed direction.

00:43:45.356 --> 00:43:48.525
Face diagonal, that's
this one here, right?

00:43:48.525 --> 00:43:55.232
So it's a root 2 over 4, right?

00:43:55.232 --> 00:43:58.769
Because again, I've got
a radius and another

00:43:58.769 --> 00:44:00.604
and another and another.

00:44:00.604 --> 00:44:04.475
There's two diameters worth
that go across that face.

00:44:04.475 --> 00:44:09.079
And so that's going to be
cubed, OK, divided by a-cubed.

00:44:09.079 --> 00:44:10.748
Now, is there something missing?

00:44:10.748 --> 00:44:11.782
Yes.

00:44:11.782 --> 00:44:14.752
The number of atoms.

00:44:14.752 --> 00:44:19.189
If you look at a FCC crystal,
and you look at the corners,

00:44:19.189 --> 00:44:22.593
each one of those shares
with eight other unit cells,

00:44:22.593 --> 00:44:23.227
so there's 1/8.

00:44:23.227 --> 00:44:26.664
That gives you the one,
like in our simple cubic.

00:44:26.664 --> 00:44:29.767
But now you've got all these
ones sharing across faces.

00:44:29.767 --> 00:44:33.504
You've got six of those,
each one sharing 1/2 atom.

00:44:33.504 --> 00:44:34.772
So that's three more.

00:44:34.772 --> 00:44:35.539
So there's 4.

00:44:38.342 --> 00:44:45.516
And this gives you a very
nice packing of 74%--

00:44:45.516 --> 00:44:46.617
74%.

00:44:46.617 --> 00:44:49.687
Let's see what our video
guy says about FCC.

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:53.857
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:44:53.857 --> 00:44:57.361
- The face-centered cubic unit
cell has a particle at each

00:44:57.361 --> 00:45:01.699
corner and in each face,
which are colored yellow here,

00:45:01.699 --> 00:45:03.434
but none in the center.

00:45:03.434 --> 00:45:06.336
The corner particles
don't touch each other.

00:45:06.336 --> 00:45:09.606
But each corner does touch
a particle in the face.

00:45:09.606 --> 00:45:13.310
And those in the faces
touch each other as well.

00:45:13.310 --> 00:45:15.979
The actual unit cell
consists of portions

00:45:15.979 --> 00:45:19.450
of particles at the
corners and in the faces.

00:45:19.450 --> 00:45:22.853
Eight-eighths at the
corners gives one particle.

00:45:22.853 --> 00:45:25.489
And half a particle
in each of six faces

00:45:25.489 --> 00:45:29.993
gives three more, for a
total of four particles.

00:45:29.993 --> 00:45:33.797
In this tiny portion of a
face-centered cubic array,

00:45:33.797 --> 00:45:37.634
notice that a given particle
has four nearest neighbors

00:45:37.634 --> 00:45:41.138
around it, four more
above, and four more below,

00:45:41.138 --> 00:45:45.008
for a total of 12
nearest neighbors.

00:45:45.008 --> 00:45:49.012
Stacking spheres shows how the
three cubic unit cells arise.

00:45:49.012 --> 00:45:50.114
Now we talk about packing.

00:45:50.114 --> 00:45:54.284
- Arranged a layer of spheres
in horizontal and vertical rows.

00:45:54.284 --> 00:45:58.622
Note the large, diamond-shaped
space among the particles.

00:45:58.622 --> 00:46:02.192
Placing the next layer
directly over the first

00:46:02.192 --> 00:46:06.563
gives a structure based on
the simple cubic unit cell.

00:46:06.563 --> 00:46:11.135
Those larger spaces mean an
inefficient use of space.

00:46:11.135 --> 00:46:14.972
In fact, only 52% of
the available volume

00:46:14.972 --> 00:46:18.675
is actually occupied by spheres.

00:46:18.675 --> 00:46:21.645
Because of this inefficiency,
the simple cubic unit cell

00:46:21.645 --> 00:46:23.547
is seen rarely in nature.

00:46:23.547 --> 00:46:24.648
Polonium!

00:46:24.648 --> 00:46:26.450
- A more efficient
stacking occurs

00:46:26.450 --> 00:46:29.586
if we place the second
layer over the spaces formed

00:46:29.586 --> 00:46:32.122
by the first layer
and the third layer

00:46:32.122 --> 00:46:34.892
over the spaces
formed by the second.

00:46:34.892 --> 00:46:39.630
That simple change leads to
68% of the available volume

00:46:39.630 --> 00:46:42.032
occupied by the
spheres and a structure

00:46:42.032 --> 00:46:45.702
based on the body-centered
cubic unit cell.

00:46:45.702 --> 00:46:49.206
Many metals, including
all the alkali metals,

00:46:49.206 --> 00:46:51.675
adopt this arrangement.

00:46:51.675 --> 00:46:53.243
For the most
efficient stacking--

00:46:53.243 --> 00:46:53.877
Now watch this.

00:46:53.877 --> 00:46:57.447
- --shift every other row in
the first layer so the large,

00:46:57.447 --> 00:47:01.618
diamond-shaped spaces become
smaller triangular spaces,

00:47:01.618 --> 00:47:04.922
and place the second
layer over them.

00:47:04.922 --> 00:47:07.691
Then the third layer
goes over the holes

00:47:07.691 --> 00:47:10.994
visible through the
first and second layers.

00:47:10.994 --> 00:47:15.065
In this arrangement, called
cubic closest packing,

00:47:15.065 --> 00:47:18.902
spheres occupy
74% of the volume.

00:47:18.902 --> 00:47:20.204
Note that it is based--

00:47:20.204 --> 00:47:21.238
[END PLAYBACK]

00:47:21.238 --> 00:47:24.741
--on the FCC crystal,
Bravais lattice.

00:47:24.741 --> 00:47:26.176
That's what he
meant to say there.

00:47:26.176 --> 00:47:28.478
And you really see it
come to life, right?

00:47:28.478 --> 00:47:30.781
I wish I could do this
somehow with the 3D models.

00:47:30.781 --> 00:47:33.250
But you stare at the 3D models
and you see that-- you know,

00:47:33.250 --> 00:47:36.053
what's interesting is
the way you put it--

00:47:36.053 --> 00:47:38.522
you slide-- this is what Hooke
did with the cannonballs.

00:47:38.522 --> 00:47:40.190
You slide that base
layer, and then you

00:47:40.190 --> 00:47:41.592
can stack them differently.

00:47:41.592 --> 00:47:45.462
But it's still a cubic
system, FCC, all right?

00:47:45.462 --> 00:47:48.198
We'll pick up on
this on Wednesday.

